        "id": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1092512/",
        "link": "https://lwn.net/Articles/1092512/",
        "relevance": 3,
        "source": "LWN.net Front Page",
        "summary": "The European Laboratory for Particle Physics, usually just called CERN , is not only the birthplace of\nthe World Wide Web , it is home to the Large Hadron\nCollider (LHC), the world's largest and highest-energy particle\naccelerator . As such, its computing environment is both truly unique and of\ngreat interest to people outside of CERN who hope to find lessons applicable to\ntheir own computing needs. The upcoming migration of some of CERN's systems from\nCentOS Linux to Debian, which was the topic of a talk at the recent MiniDebConf Winterthur 2026 ,\nis of particular interest.",
        "summary_vi": "CERN is transitioning its unique computing infrastructure from CentOS Linux to Debian. This migration path offers valuable architectural and operational insights for organizations managing complex and specialized computing environments.",
        "title": "[$] CERN's migration path from CentOS Linux to Debian"
      },
      "first_seen": "2026-09-08T00:43:47.238508+00:00"
    },
    "oai:arXiv.org:2609.04238v1": {
      "entry": {
        "category": "research",
        "id": "oai:arXiv.org:2609.04238v1",
        "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04238",
        "relevance": 3,
        "source": "arXiv cs.AR (Computer Architecture)",
        "summary": "arXiv:2609.04238v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: Autoregressive decoding on cheap hardware is bound not by FLOPs but by the bytes each generated token must move across the slowest populated tier of a memory hierarchy. We treat bytes-per-token as a first-class design axis, organized by an address-determinism taxonomy that classifies parameters by when their fetch address becomes known during a token's forward pass (A0: at token sampling; A1: before attention; A2: layerwise data-dependent; A3: always read). This reduces prefetch scheduling to single-machine feasibility with release times, yielding a closed-form windowed roofline. We run dual-budget (bytes-per-token times storage) ablations across three sub-100M scales, then take the framework to real large-MoE deployment and report a substantial negative result the roofline predicts: on an 8GB edge board running Qwen3-30B-A3B (4-bit, 18GB), the model overflows RAM and decode is pinned at the eMMC bandwidth ceiling; predictive expert prefetch does not help -- not temporal-locality prefetch (net-negative), not even a trace-driven oracle with perfect prediction -- because the binding constraint is byte volume over a saturated bus, which prefetch cannot reduce. The lever that works is reducing bytes-per-token until the model fits the fast tier: quantized to fit a 16GB unified-memory device, the same model runs GPU-resident at 11.5 tok/s (22x). We reconcile this with GPU-serving expert-prefetch predictors: a frozen-model probe predicts Qwen3-30B routing from the pre-attention state at 91.2%, a scale-invariant predictability property, but this converts to throughput only where the fast tier caches most of the model and per-token transfer is comparable to compute -- measured to hold on an A100 PCIe-offload path and to fail on bandwidth-walled edge storage. Predictability is not speedup; we chart where the gap closes.",
        "summary_vi": "This paper analyzes autoregressive LLM decoding on low-cost hardware by focusing on the bytes-per-token memory bandwidth bottleneck. It introduces a windowed storage roofline model and dual-budget architectures to optimize data movement across memory hierarchies.",
        "title": "Budgeting Bytes: A Windowed Storage Roofline and Dual-Budget Architecture Ablations for Storage-Bound LLM Decoding"
      },
      "first_seen": "2026-09-08T00:43:47.238508+00:00"
    },
    "oai:arXiv.org:2609.04774v1": {
      "entry": {
        "category": "research",
        "id": "oai:arXiv.org:2609.04774v1",
        "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.04774",
        "relevance": 3,
        "source": "arXiv cs.OS (Operating Systems)",
        "summary": "arXiv:2609.04774v1 Announce Type: new \nAbstract: As LLM context windows expand and input sequences grow longer, serving systems face increasing computational and memory demands. Context parallelism (CP), which partitions the input sequence across multiple ranks to parallelize the computation, has therefore become increasingly important for efficient LLM serving. However, existing CP-enabled systems either rely on static CP configurations or adjust the CP degree only for active requests or batches. In this paper, we present Vertumnus, an adaptive CP serving system designed for heterogeneous and evolving workloads. At the request level, Vertumnus routes requests among workers with different CP degrees using a placement cost that combines predicted queuing delay, cache-aware prefill time, and GPU-time cost. At the cluster level, Vertumnus adapts the worker composition through seconds-scale split and merge operations as workload demand changes. Vertumnus further introduces a global prefix-cache management policy that coordinates cache placement and replication among workers with the same or different CP degrees, preserving cache locality as request assignments and worker composition change. Experiments on a 64-GPU cluster with public and production workloads show that, under the highest evaluated loads, Vertumnus reduces mean TTFT by up to 28.1% and improves token-weighted SLO attainment by up to 13.3 percentage points over the strongest baseline.",
        "summary_vi": "The authors introduce an adaptive context parallelism framework designed to optimize LLM serving for long-context requests. The system dynamically scales the parallelism degree to match varying memory and computational workloads in production.",
        "title": "Adaptive Context Parallelism for Production LLM Serving"
      },
      "first_seen": "2026-09-08T00:43:47.238508+00:00"
    }
  },
